qa(None, "What is cross-sample validation, why did you run it in both directions, and how is it different from cross-dataset validation?",
   f"The study contains two groups of women who never overlap: {szv('cross_to_long', 'train_women')} women with one session and {szv('cross_to_long', 'test_women')} women with up to three sessions. In direction 1 I trained on the single-session women and tested on the longitudinal women; in direction 2 the reverse. Running both directions shows whether a result depends on which group is used to learn. It is stronger than leave-one-woman-out because the test women come from a different recruitment design, but it is not cross-dataset validation: both groups come from the same study (presumably the same laboratory, assays and recruitment period), so shared peculiarities of that study would not be detected. Only a second, independent dataset could support a claim of generalisation across studies; I looked for one and found none that is open and compatible (docs/cross_dataset_candidates.md).")
qa(None, "How did you prevent leakage in the cross-sample experiment?",
   f"Four safeguards. (1) An assertion in the code stops the run if any woman appears in both the training and the test group (women in both: {szv('cross_to_long', 'women_in_both')} in direction 1 and {szv('long_to_cross', 'women_in_both')} in direction 2). (2) Every quantity learned from data uses the training group only: the mean and standard deviation for scaling, the baseline mean, and the hyper-parameters, which are chosen by an inner 5-fold cross-validation grouped by woman inside the training group. (3) No test outcome or test feature statistic enters any step. (4) The scikit-learn ridge and my from-scratch ridge give the same predictions (largest difference {agg(E7['ridge_parity'], '', 'max_abs_pred_diff', 'max', '{:.1e}', label='E7 ridge parity max diff')}), and direction 1 reproduces the Phase 4 cross-sample predictions exactly, which shows that the pipeline is the same one as before.")
qa(None, "Why was the skill different in the two directions?",
   f"For the questionnaire model (DRSP, set D, ridge) R2 against the training mean was {ci(E7['metrics'], dict(sample='cross_to_long', target='drsp_psych', model='ridge', fset='D'), 'R2_oos', '{:+.3f}', label='E7 ridge D drsp dir1')} when testing on the longitudinal women and {ci(E7['metrics'], dict(sample='long_to_cross', target='drsp_psych', model='ridge', fset='D'), 'R2_oos', '{:+.3f}', label='E7 ridge D drsp dir2')} when testing on the single-session women. Candidate explanations from the shift analysis: the longitudinal women vary more (SD ratio of test to training {calc('E7 min SD ratio dir1', float(SS[SS.direction == 'cross_to_long'].sd_ratio_test_over_train.min()), '{:.2f}', E7['shift_summary'], 'min SD ratio, cross_to_long')} to {calc('E7 max SD ratio dir1', float(SS[SS.direction == 'cross_to_long'].sd_ratio_test_over_train.max()), '{:.2f}', E7['shift_summary'], 'max SD ratio, cross_to_long')} in direction 1 versus {calc('E7 min SD ratio dir2', float(SS[SS.direction == 'long_to_cross'].sd_ratio_test_over_train.min()), '{:.2f}', E7['shift_summary'], 'min SD ratio, long_to_cross')} to {calc('E7 max SD ratio dir2', float(SS[SS.direction == 'long_to_cross'].sd_ratio_test_over_train.max()), '{:.2f}', E7['shift_summary'], 'max SD ratio, long_to_cross')} in direction 2), the training samples differ in size, and the hormone-phase relation differs. The mean level of the targets does not explain it (at most {calc('E7 max mean shift share', float(SS.mean_shift_share_of_baseline_mse.max()), '{:.3f}', E7['shift_summary'], 'max share')} of the baseline error). With only two groups I cannot separate these causes, so they remain suggestions.")
qa(None, "Did hormones help in either direction, and what do the Holm and FDR corrections change?",
   f"No. Over all {calc('E7 number of hormone tests', int(len(HT)), '{:.0f}', E7['hormone_tests'], 'rows')} hormone comparisons the raw p-values below 0.05 were {agg(E7['hormone_tests'], 'p_two_sided < 0.05', None, 'count', '{:.0f}', label='E7 raw sig all')}; after Holm {agg(E7['hormone_tests'], 'p_holm_all < 0.05', None, 'count', '{:.0f}', label='E7 Holm sig all')} and after FDR {agg(E7['hormone_tests'], 'p_fdr_all < 0.05', None, 'count', '{:.0f}', label='E7 FDR sig all')} remained, and the adjusted one-sided p-value for an improvement was never below {calc('E7 min Holm improve all', float(HT.p_holm_all_improve.min()), '{:.3f}', E7['hormone_tests'], 'min p_holm_all_improve')} (Holm) or {calc('E7 min FDR improve all', float(HT.p_fdr_all_improve.min()), '{:.3f}', E7['hormone_tests'], 'min p_fdr_all_improve')} (FDR). Holm controls the chance of at least one false positive and is valid under any dependence; Benjamini-Hochberg FDR controls the expected share of false discoveries and is more permissive. Here the two agree. The uncorrected 'significant' results were mostly cases where hormones made predictions worse in direction 1 ({agg(E7['hormone_tests'], Q1 + ' and ci_lo > 0', None, 'count', '{:.0f}', label='E7 dir1 C vs A worse')} of {agg(E7['hormone_tests'], Q1, None, 'count', '{:.0f}', label='E7 dir1 C vs A tests')} comparisons).")
qa(None, "How do you read a standardised mean difference and a Kolmogorov-Smirnov test, and which shifts did you find?",
   f"The standardised mean difference (SMD) is the difference between the group means in units of the pooled standard deviation (about 0.2 small, 0.5 medium); the Kolmogorov-Smirnov statistic is the largest gap between the two cumulative distributions (0 = identical, 1 = fully separate). Sessions of one woman are not independent, so I tested the first session of each woman ({val(E7['shift_variables'], dict(variable='BDI_RW'), 'n_first_cross', '{:.0f}', label='E7 n first cross BDI')} versus {val(E7['shift_variables'], dict(variable='BDI_RW'), 'n_first_long', '{:.0f}', label='E7 n first long BDI')} women) and corrected for the {calc('E7 number of variables compared', int(len(SV)), '{:.0f}', E7['shift_variables'], 'rows')} variables with Holm: no test was significant (smallest adjusted p {calc('E7 min KS Holm', float(SV.ks_p_first_holm.min()), '{:.3f}', E7['shift_variables'], 'min ks_p_first_holm')}). The SMD intervals exclude 0 only for the peri-ovulatory share and for log progesterone (SMD {val(E7['shift_variables'], dict(variable='logP'), 'smd_long_minus_cross', '{:+.2f}', label='E7 SMD logP')}); progesterone is higher in the longitudinal women in each phase, which is why a classifier separates the groups when hormones and phase are combined (AUC {ci(E7['shift_auc'], dict(fset='C'), 'auc', '{:.2f}', lo='auc_lo', hi='auc_hi', label='E7 AUC C')}). Failing to detect a shift does not prove there is none, since the groups are small.")
